# Patterned Deposition of Metal-Organic Frameworks onto Plastic, Paper, and Textile Substrates by Inkjet Printing of a Precursor Solution

## abstract
Flexible in many aspects: inkjet printing of metal-organic frameworks permits their larger area, high-resolution deposition in any desired pattern, even in the form of gradients or shades. When flexible substrates are used, many applications can be envisioned, such as sensing and capture of hazardous gases for personal safety measures.

Dedicated to Prof. Dr. Werner Uhl on the occasion of his 60th birthday
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted considerable interest because of their high surface areas, tunable pore size, and chemical versatility. 1  These materials already have demonstrated potential for gas storage/separation, 2  heterogeneous catalysis, 3  drug delivery, 4  and sensing. 5  For their application as smart membranes, catalytic coatings, or sensing devices, the spatially and morphologically controlled growth of MOF films on various surfaces gained significant importance. 5,6  In order to produce such surface-attached MOFs (so-called SURMOFs), a number of interesting methods have been developed, including direct (hydrothermal) growth, 7  seeded growth, 8  dip-coating, 9 layer-by-layer growth, 10  electrochemical deposition, 11  gellayer deposition, 12  spray-coating, 13  and evaporation/solvent induced growth. 14  While these methods mostly yield uniform films, the precise positioning of laterally structured SURMOFs is crucial for integrated device fabrication. 15  Several patterning techniques, such as soft lithography, 7b 10a 14a,b 16  pen-type lithography, 14d  photolithography, 17  deep X-ray lithography, 18 and electron-beam lithography, 19  have been introduced to this area. Although such patterning techniques allow the fabrication of SURMOFs with high lateral resolution in the micro-/ nanometer regime, most of the growth methods and patterning techniques consist of complicated multistep procedures, which often require costly/complicated machinery, take long times, produce relatively large amounts of chemical waste, and are typically limited to relatively small overall areas. Furthermore, the established procedures are mainly focusing on the deposition of SURMOFs onto non-flexible, non-transparent substrates, such as metals (e.g., SAM functionalized Au, Cu) or oxides (e.g., $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ . 5 6 8 10 20  The deposition of MOFs onto plastics, papers, and textiles might extensively expand their applications, for example, as platforms for photonic or biomedical testing, since such applications benefit from the low costs, flexibility, biocompatibility, and–for plastics–transparency of these substrates. 21  To date, very few studies of MOF on such substrates have been reported, and none dealt with laterally structured SURMOFs. 22  Thus, the development of an effective and general method for the controlled deposition of SURMOF on such substrates remains a challenge.
Inkjet printing has recently attracted tremendous interest for the defined deposition of inorganic or organic materials. 23  It has been widely investigated for the printing of conducting and semiconducting materials with the aim of achieving “printed electronics”. 23,24  To the best of our knowledge, inkjet printing has not been developed yet for the fabrication of SURMOFs. Inspired by a new concept for MOFs processing, the evaporation/solvent induced crystallization 14a,b  from a stable precursor solution containing the metal, as well as the organic building blocks, we considered that such a precursor solution might also serve as “ink”. Here, we wish to demonstrate that inkjet printing, even with a commercial, off-the-shelf inkjet printer, is a straightforward route to deposit patterned SURMOFs onto various flexible substrates. As shown in Scheme 1  the MOF precursor was printed onto specific positions using a nozzle. Further drying and solvent-assisted development steps caused the formation of the respective, laterally structured SURMOFs.
In this work, we will mainly focus on the deposition of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ (btc is 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate), also known as HKUST-1, 25  but other MOFs can also be deposited using the same approach (see below). Due to the crucial role of the viscosity and surface tension of the ink for the inkjet printing process, the previously reported HKUST-1 precursor 14  turned out to be not printable with inkjet printers. Therefore, we developed a new ink system, which contained dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO), as the previously used precursor solution, as well as ethanol and ethylene glycol (EG) as solvent combination. Although our previous study indicated that pure ethanol triggers the precipitation of the MOF from the DMSO solution, 14b  the presence of EG dramatically improves the stability of the precursor solution. Even after eight months storage under ambient conditions, the solution remained light blue and no precipitation could be observed (Figure 1a). When this solution was used as ink, the desired patterns could easily be obtained by printing followed by drying in an oven. The density and thickness of the SURMOF could be simply controlled by printing the designed patterns for multiple “printing-drying” cycles (abbreviated as ncy in the following). As a final step, a solvent development was carried out by immersing the as-printed HKUST-1 patterns into a methanol solution for $30\mathrm{min}$ to remove the less volatile solvents, in particular EG, from the porous network. Figure 1b,c show a series of HKUST-1 patterns and letters on transparent PET foils after three printing-drying cycles (3cy). The image in Figure 1d gives an impression of the attainable resolution (better than $200\upmu\mathrm{m})$ ), which is basically limited by the current printer. Note that the inkjet process not only allows us to obtain large and complex SURMOF patterns, but also to create SURMOF gradients by simply defining a color gradient (e.g., from black to grey, Figure 1b). It would be extremely complicated to obtain such SURMOF gradients by using other patterning techniques. 15
To learn more about the printed SURMOF, surface X-ray diffraction (SXRD), Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), and scanning electron microscopy (SEM) were used. For this, 1cy to 8cy of HKUST-1 were printed onto foils (Figure S1, Supporting Information). Interestingly, the printed HKUST-1 film was still semi-transparent even after 8cy. The chemical identity of the HKUST-1 films was confirmed by comparison of the SXRD pattern with the simulated X-ray diffractogram (Figure 2c and Figure S2, Supporting Information) as well as of the surface FTIR with the attenuated total reflection (ATR) FTIR spectrum of the bulk phase (Figure 2d and Figure S3, Supporting Information), which all show good agreement. SEM images (Figure 2e,f) indicate that the printed area was basically completely covered by HKUST-1 particles, even after 2cy. From SEM cross-section images (Figure S4, Supporting Information), the thickness of the HKUST-1 films was estimated to be about $6~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ after 8cy. Gravimetry indicated the deposition of $0.9\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ which corresponds to a MOF to substrate ratio of 1 to 15 (see Supporting Information for details).
In contrast to previous reports, where the HKUST-1 crystals usually showed well-defined shapes—typically (truncated) octahedra— 14  we observed for the printed HKUST-1 crystals no specific shape. In particular, after several printing cycles, the films were rather composed of densely grown, non-oriented crystals, in which individual crystals hardly could be distinguished. This might be caused by the fast nucleation during the thermal treatment at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ To test this, we used a slow nucleation procedure as described in our previous work. 14b  For this, the printed substrate was loaded into a desiccator containing MeOH vapor and kept for $15\mathrm{min}$ after each printing step. As shown in Figure S5, Supporting Information, this slower nucleation indeed leads to the formation of (truncated) octahedral crystals after 3cy. SXRD indicates that these films now show a preferred [111] orientation, what goes in line with previous findings. 14b
While the formation of oriented, well-defined crystals might be preferable in some cases, the procedure used here rather offers another interesting morphological feature. As the high resolution SEM images (insets in Figure 2e,f) show, the HKUST-1 films deposited by inkjet printing, followed by the wet development, expose a pore structure with pore sizes in the range of $10{-}50~\mathrm{nm}$ , thus being located in the mesoporous regime. The
