combination of these mesopores with the intrinsic micropores of the MOF results in a very efficient hierarchy, which improves the adsorption behavior of the system by increasing the diffusion of guest molecules into the bulk, while the benefits of the high internal surface of the micropores are maintained (see below). 26  Recently a surfactant-templating method has been described for the synthesis of such a mesoporous HKUST-1 bulk material, 27  but not for thin film fabrication. In our case, we found that both the presence of the EG in the ink solution, as well as the solvent development step, are essential to create such mesopores. Without EG, the ink not only is unstable, but the precipitate shows no mesopores, as demonstrated earlier. 14b  In the printed HKUST-1 films, the mesopores only appear after the treatment with methanol (see Figure S6, Supporting Information), which causes the complete removal of EG from the SURMOF, as can be demonstrated by FTIR (Supporting Information, Figure S3, grey areas). Obviously, part of the material either is removed or collapses upon the removal of the EG, creating the mesopores, and thus forming the hierarchical structure. According to the SXRD results, this reorganization proceeds without significantly changing the crystal structure (Figure S2, Supporting Information).
The inkjet printing of HKUST-1 SURMOFs was successfully performed not only on plastics, but also on various other flexible substrates, such as a variety of papers as well as textiles (Figure 3 and Supporting Information, Figure S7). The SEM image in Figure 3c clearly displays the growth of HKUST-1 crystals on the fiber of office paper after printing 8cy (for comparison, see Figure S8, Supporting Information). As with the SURMOFs on foil, we again observed a hierarchical pore structure of HKUST-1 crystals after the solvent development step (Figure 3d). However, the density of HKUST-1 crystals on paper is rather low compared to the one on foil with the same number of printing cycles. This can be explained by considering the complex morphology of paper, which consists of crosslinked fibers causing high roughness and large voids between the fibers. These voids can serve as reservoirs for the ink so that the precursor solution accumulates in there rather than on the surface of the fibers. Indeed, SEM in Figure S9, Supporting Information, shows that these voids were filled with agglomerates of HKUST-1 crystals.
One of the most interesting features of HKUST-1 is that the framework contains a high density of accessible, Lewisacidic copper(II) sites which can coordinate small molecules. 28 Typically, the color of printed HKUST-1 films is blue-turquoise under atmosphere due to water molecules coordinating to the open copper sites. The dehydration of HKUST-1 film can be easily attained by using vacuum or blowing a stream of $\mathrm{\tilde{N}}_{2}$ over it. This dehydration is accompanied by a pronounced color change from blue-turquoise to deep violet (Supporting Information, Figures S10,S11). 28a,b  Yaghi and co-workers have demonstrated that bulk HKUST-1 shows great dynamic gas-adsorption properties, particularly for the irreversible adsorption of ammonia $(8.7~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , which was found to be 100 times higher than for activated carbon. 28a  In contrast to bulk materials, immobilized MOFs, especially on textiles, are considered to be more versatile and convenient for the personal protection applications, for example, serving as gas mask or working clothes. 22b,d,e 28a
This has driven us to test the ability of our printed HKUST-1 on textile to capture hazardous gases. As shown in Figure 4a, the original HKUST-1 rapidly changes its color from turquoise to dark blue, yellow, and brown after exposure to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ HCl, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ vapor, respectively. This guest-molecule, dependent color change, not only indicates the capture process, but also presents the possibility of inkjet printed SURMOF serving as label-free, inexpensive, and practical gas sensors.
To confirm that the hierarchical HKUST-1 film fabricated from “ink” precursor solution are in fact still microporous and thus capable to capture guest molecules, we deposited these films onto the electrodes of quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) sensors (Figure S12, Supporting Information). Due to the sensitivity of QCM sensors, they are conveniently used to study the adsorption properties of MOF films. 11a 29  In our case, the activation by simple purging with $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ (in an otherwise empty desiccator) led to a $35\mathrm{-}38\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ loss of water indicating a high accessible pore volume of the HKUST-1 SURMOF. When the flask was filled with the target gas (e.g., $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ ), a dramatic increase of mass was observed leveling off after about a minute due to saturation (Figure 4b and Supporting Information, Figures S13,S14). The adsorption capacities of the HKUST-1 films for various gases are displayed in Figure 4c. Except for oxygen, the HKUST-1 films show good adsorption properties for the tested gases. In case of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , the adsorption was completely reversible (Figure S14, Supporting Information), while for most other gases the adsorption process was irreversible (e.g., HCl, see Figure S13, Supporting Information). Interestingly, the adsorption of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ was partially reversible (Figure 4b) meaning that the ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}$ molecules do not only bind to the unsaturated copper sites $(17.1~\mathrm{wt}\%)$ , but also become physically adsorbed $(10.1~\mathrm{{wt\%}})$ in the channels of HKUST-1 crystals. Surprisingly, this behavior does not strictly correlate with the sustainment of the MOF structure: while the SXRD of the SURMOF does not change upon adsorption of $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ no diffraction peaks could be found after the adsorption of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ HCl, or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , indicating significant chemical transformations (Supporting Information, Figures S15,S16). This should not hamper the use of this materials, for example, in personal protection gear, since such items are typically single-use only.
In conclusion, we have developed a new, facile, and precursor-based strategy for the growth of SURMOFs on various flexible substrates (plastics, papers, and textiles) by using the inkjet-printing technique. This unique method allows us to fabricate large area, high density, and laterally patterned SURMOFs (including gradients) in a low cost, time-efficient way. The printed HKUST-1 films are characterized by a hierarchical, surfactant-free pore structure, what makes them promising materials for gas capture and heterogeneous catalysis, since the diffusion of guest molecules becomes facilitated. 27  HKUST-1 SURMOFs printed on textiles are able to capture several hazardous gases and indicate this process by a unique color change, which might be useful for personal protection applications. The high accessible pore volume of HKUST-1 films was confirmed by QCM measurements and resulted in excellent adsorption properties for several hazardous gases. In view of serving as platforms for sensing and biomedical testing, SURMOFs printed on plastics and papers are expected to be excellent candidates.
To demonstrate the generality of this approach, we also printed patterns of a MOF from a completely different family, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{adc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})_{2}]$ $\mathtt{\langle a d c\ =\ 9,10}$ -anthracenedicarboxylate, dabco $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 1{,}4$ -diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane, see Figure S17, Supporting Information). The fluorescence of this MOF has been reported to be sensitive against different organic guest molecules, 30 expanding the opportunities for inkjet printed SURMOF sensing systems. Recent publication of several other homogeneous precursor solutions for different MOFs 14a 31  and ZIFs (zeolitic imidazole frameworks) 14a  open the field further for many potential applications.
